Given that the universal format-to-format translator
{Pandoc} is coming of age, \LaTeX{} authors are tempted
to think that whatever \LaTeX{} they write can quickly be
translated without worry to whatever other format may be
required.

Of course, that is not exactly true, but the use of an
XML{} profile of \LaTeX{} can make it exactly true.
However, an SGML{} profile of \LaTeX{} can provide
closer emulation of classical \LaTeX{} than an XML{}
profile.

Most actors in the world of markup have restricted their use
of SGML{} to XML{}.  For that reason software that
handles SGML{} beyond the realm of XML{} seems to
be falling out of maintenance.  If the \LaTeX{} community
wishes to continue to be able to avail itself of the
advantages of SGML{} for \LaTeX{} source emulation, it
may fall on the \LaTeX{} community to maintain the extant
SGML{} libraries.
